.Dd November 15, 2026
.Dt KAIGI 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kaigi
.Nd administer the Kaigi conferencing service on FreeBSD
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm service
.Cm kaigi
.Ar start | stop | restart | status
.Op Ar instance
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Kaigi is the FreeGateway Project's self-hosted video conferencing service,
derived from MiroTalk SFU and built on mediasoup.
Every participant authenticates and receives access to explicitly authorized
meetings. Browser resources are served locally.
Kaigi uses its own service, configuration, runtime and log directories,
allowing a separate MiroTalk installation to coexist.
.Pp
Kaigi supports self-hosted, open-source, administrator-controlled infrastructure.
Integrations that require third-party-controlled services are not supported.
.Pp
The
.Nm
port installs Kaigi under
.Pa %%WWWDIR%%
and integrates it with the FreeBSD rc system.
Enable it with:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kaigi_enable="YES"
kaigi_instances="staff"
.Ed
.Pp
The rc.conf interface is intentionally limited to service
execution:
.Bl -tag -width "kaigi_daemon_group"
.It Va kaigi_enable
Enable the service.  Default:
.Dq NO .
.It Va kaigi_daemon_user
Service user passed to
.Xr daemon 8 .  Default:
.Dq www .
.It Va kaigi_daemon_group
Group used for named-instance state directories.  Default:
.Dq www .
.It Va kaigi_instances
Required, nonempty space-separated instance names. There is no default instance.
Names start with a lowercase letter, contain only lowercase letters, digits
and underscores, and have at most 32 characters.
Every name uses the same configuration, state, PID, log and cookie rules.
.It Va kaigi_start_timeout
Readiness deadline in seconds, from 1 through 300. Default:
.Dq 30 .
.El
.Sh NAMED INSTANCES
All configuration belongs below
.Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/instances/NAME ,
including deployments with only one instance. Replace NAME with the chosen name.
The package installs templates and a provisioning README under
.Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/instances/example .
No template is enabled automatically and no active configuration, account or JWT
secret is installed. Copy the example to a new named directory, rename required
.sample files and configure the deployment before starting. Package upgrades
update only the examples, not administrator-created instance files.
.Pp
For example, configure two independent instances in
.Pa /etc/rc.conf :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kaigi_enable="YES"
kaigi_instances="staff public"
.Ed
.Pp
An action without an instance argument operates on every configured instance.
An action with an instance argument operates only on that configured instance:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
service kaigi start
service kaigi restart staff
service kaigi status public
service kaigi stop
.Ed
.Pp
Each named instance reads its own configuration, accounts, secret, translations,
meeting properties, LDAP settings and public assets below
.Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/instances/NAME .
Provision this directory before starting it; there is no top-level configuration
fallback. Keep users and jwt-secret root-owned mode 0600. Configuration
and public assets must be readable by the selected service account.
.Pp
Configure a distinct web listening port and public URL, and disjoint media port
ranges, in every instance's kaigi.conf. For example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
WEB_LISTEN_IP=192.0.2.10
WEB_LISTEN_PORT=7443
WEB_URL=https://meetings.example.org:7443
MEDIA_LISTEN_IP=192.0.2.10
MEDIA_ANNOUNCED_IP=192.0.2.10
MEDIA_MIN_PORT=40000
MEDIA_MAX_PORT=40100
MEDIA_NUM_WORKERS=2
.Ed
.Pp
A second instance might use web port 8443 and media ports 40200 through 40300.
Account for TCP as well as UDP media listeners, firewall rules and any configured
relay. Sharing a certificate is possible when it covers the hostname.
.Pp
Named instances use
.Pa /var/run/kaigi/NAME/kaigi.pid ,
.Pa /var/log/kaigi/NAME/kaigi.log
and
.Pa /var/db/kaigi/NAME .
Recordings default to the recordings subdirectory of that state directory.
For a named instance, RECORDING_DIR is relative to its state directory, or an
absolute path within it; paths outside that directory are rejected.
.Pp
Optional per-instance account overrides are:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kaigi_staff_daemon_user="kaigi_staff"
kaigi_staff_daemon_group="kaigi_staff"
.Ed
.Pp
Create accounts separately. The daemon user's account determines process group
membership; daemon_group controls the state directory's group, not setgid.
PID and log directories remain root-owned. State directories are mode 0700.
Each instance creates its own secret. Never copy credentials or secrets
implicitly from another instance.
.Pp
Ports separate browser origins, but not the browser's cookie scope or same-site
boundary. Named instances use different navigation cookie names to prevent
accidental session replacement. This does not isolate mutually untrusted sites;
use separate hostnames and service accounts, or jails, for stronger isolation.
Instances using the same account share that account's filesystem privileges.
.Pp
Start and restart perform a locked service-wide preflight before changing any
process. This checks every configured instance, including current live allocation
snapshots, for conflicting TCP/UDP ports and configuration/state paths. It validates
configuration, credentials, service accounts and TLS under the intended account.
Hostname resolution uses the same code as the running server. A validation failure
leaves running instances untouched. Worker counts must be positive integers;
excessive aggregate counts produce a warning.
.Pp
Readiness requires initialized workers, a listening HTTP server and a fresh
per-launch acknowledgment matching the Node PID and process start time. Failure
returns nonzero and signals only the failed launch's supervisor. After five
seconds without exit it forcibly stops that launch's validated child and
supervisor; cleanup can add five seconds to the readiness deadline. Remaining
instances are not started/restarted after a runtime failure. Operations are
sequential, not transactional; runtime failures can occur after successful
preflight. Already running or successfully started instances remain untouched.
.Pp
The service discards inherited caller environment overrides; use kaigi.conf.
NODE_ENV defaults to production. Startup metadata is root-owned and contains
resource allocations, PID and process start time, not credentials. Pre-safety
running instances lack allocation snapshots: stop them once before starting
other instances. A single-instance restart can validate its replacement first.
Group restart also checks old allocations that remain live until their turn;
stop the affected group before reallocating ranges that would conflict during
sequential restart.
.Pp
Explicit stop/status works after removal from the instance list and without
valid application configuration. Stop without a name also discovers PID
directories, ignoring malformed configured names. A stale recorded process
identity is rejected instead of signaling an unrelated process.
Stopping an instance does not delete its configuration or recordings.
.Sh APPLICATION CONFIGURATION
Use
.Xr kaigi.conf 5
for deployment, site, and application environment settings.
The application configuration code at
.Pa %%WWWDIR%%/app/src/config.js
is package-owned and is replaced on upgrades.
Do not edit it to configure the service.
Administrator settings belong in
.Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/instances/NAME/kaigi.conf .
Kaigi does not read or migrate settings from a MiroTalk installation.
.Sh SITE ASSETS
Site-owned logos and other presentation files may be stored in
.Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/instances/NAME/site-assets
and are served under
.Pa /site-assets/ .
Kaigi serves these files directly from the selected configuration directory,
without a shared public-directory symlink.
The administrator-owned
.Pa site.css
is loaded after Kaigi's packaged stylesheets so local CSS can adjust such things
as logo dimensions without patching package-owned CSS.
.Pp
The unauthenticated
.Pa /about
page identifies the site and upstream open-source software and provides the
Corresponding Source link for the exact FreeBSD package revision.
.Sh LOCAL USERS
Local authentication is enabled by default in
.Xr kaigi.conf 5 .
The account database is
.Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/instances/NAME/users
and should be owned by
.Li root:wheel
with mode 0600.
.Pp
Each account occupies one line with four fields separated by
.Ql | :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
username | password | display name | allowed rooms
.Ed
.Pp
Whitespace around fields is ignored.
Blank lines and lines whose first non-whitespace character is
.Ql #
are ignored.
The
.Ql |
character and newline cannot occur in a field.
The allowed-rooms field is either
.Ql *
or a JSON array of exact meeting keys.
For example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
fred    | H7mQ9kR4WxPc | Fred Bloggs | *
mary    | N8vK2pT7YsQa | Mary Smith  | ["staff", "weekly"]
.Ed
.Pp
A template is installed as
.Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/instances/example/users.sample .
.Sh JWT SECRET
On first start the rc script creates
.Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/instances/NAME/jwt-secret
from 32 bytes of kernel-provided random data.
The resulting 256-bit secret is root-owned, mode 0600, and reused on later
starts.
Kaigi consumes it during configuration and removes
.Ev JWT_SECRET
from the Node process environment.
.Sh LOGGING
Standard output and error are written to
.Pa /var/log/kaigi/NAME/kaigi.log .
The package installs a
.Xr newsyslog 8
configuration to rotate this log rather than retaining it indefinitely.
Named-instance rotation entries are generated under
.Pa /var/run/kaigi/newsyslog
and included by the installed newsyslog configuration.
Rotation signals each instance's daemon supervisor to reopen its log, not the
Node process. The supervisor PID is stored alongside kaigi.pid in daemon.pid.
Point any fail2ban jail at the relevant instance log and scope its firewall
action to that instance's listening ports if bans should remain independent.
The supplied filter and PF/IPFW setup instructions are installed under
.Pa %%PREFIX%%/share/kaigi/fail2ban .
Fail2ban is optional and must be configured and enabled by the administrator.
On a jail host, run it on the host with access to the instance log and firewall.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "%%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/instances/example/kaigi.conf.sample"
.It Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/instances/NAME/kaigi.conf
Deployment and site configuration.
.It Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/instances/example/kaigi.conf.sample
Package-provided deployment example.
.It Pa %%WWWDIR%%/app/src/config.js
Package-owned application configuration code.
.It Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/instances/NAME/users
Root-only local user database.
.It Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/instances/example/users.sample
Example local user database.
.It Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/instances/NAME/site-assets
Site-owned presentation assets.
.It Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/instances/NAME/jwt-secret
Automatically generated JWT signing secret.
.It Pa /var/run/kaigi/NAME
Runtime state and pid file.
.It Pa /var/log/kaigi/NAME/kaigi.log
Service log.
.El
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
Kaigi's security model, administrator choices and audit limits are documented in
.Pa %%DOCSDIR%%/HARDENING.md .
.Pp
The files
.Pa users
and
.Pa jwt-secret
contain secrets and should remain root-owned mode 0600.
.Pp
.Pa kaigi.conf
is read as configuration data by Kaigi and is not executed as shell code.
Do not place user passwords or the JWT secret in it or in
.Pa rc.conf .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kaigi.conf 5 ,
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
.Xr service 8
.Sh PRIVACY
The site administrator defines the deployment's privacy policy.
A site privacy policy link is optional and is configured with
.Va PRIVACY_POLICY_NAME
or
.Va PRIVACY_POLICY_URL
in
.Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/instances/NAME/kaigi.conf .
If neither is configured, the user interface does not display a Privacy Policy link.
.Sh SITE COPYRIGHT
Site-specific material may have a different copyright owner from the Kaigi
software and its upstream components.  Set
.Va SITE_COPYRIGHT
in
.Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/instances/NAME/kaigi.conf
to display an optional site copyright notice in the login footer and local
About page. Kaigi and upstream software attribution, copyright, license
and Corresponding Source information remain on the About page.
